  "query_preview": "..\n\nRest of Today through next Tuesday...\n\nWeak ridging over the area today keeping quiet and mostly sunny\nconditions over the area this afternoon. A disturbance crossing S.\nCanada just north of the Montana border is pushing a weak\nnortherly wind shift through the area at this time, with a\nreinforcement of the cooler air we have seen over the past few\ndays. Generally light winds tonight may allow patchy fog to\ndevelop over river valleys tonight, mainly east of Billings.\nOtherwise a quiet and seasonally cool night anticipated across the\nregion. \n\nLow level winds turn southeasterly tomorrow and work to advect in\nplains moisture to the area through the day. At the same time a\ndeepening upper low over NorCal will drift this direction, with\nenergy rotating around the eastern side of the low pushing up into\nour area by mid afternoon. This energy will be enough for isolated\nto scattered showers and a few thunderstorms to develop over\nsouthern zones during the afternoon hours. Will have to keep an\neye on convection over SE Montana through Thursday evening where \nthe strongest moisture advection arrives during this period with a few\nmodels showing locally heavy rainfall not too far away from \nCarter/Fallon counties. PWAT values climb to 200% of normal\nThursday night.  \n\nThere may be a few showers lingering overnight into Friday\nmorning, but overall this period looks to be a break before\nstronger forcing and more widespread precipitation develops for\nFriday afternoon through Saturday night. There is a period Friday\nafternoon into Friday evening where shear and CAPE profiles\ncombine to provide the potential for a few strong to possibly\nsevere thunderstorms. Models are not in great agreement with\nexactly where the highest potential for strong storms will be, but\nareas just east of Billings seem to be the area models want to get\nstorms going early to mid afternoon, slowly spreading eastward\nthrough mid evening. AI/ML guidance has a 5 to 15 percent severe\nthreat over this area. SPC will likely wait to see a better model\nconsensus as the hi-res CAM models start to advertise this period\nbefore adjusting the general thunderstorm outlook upward. So, if\nyou have outdoor plans Friday afternoon, be prepared for\nthunderstorm activity, with a few strong storms (hail/strong\nwinds/very heavy rainfall) possible. \n\nPrecipitable water values reach 200-225% of normal Friday \nafternoon through Saturday across the entire forecast area. This \nmoisture will present the potential for locally heavy rainfall \nduring this period. Latest model runs continue to move the slow \nmoving NorCal low in a favorable track for a widespread long \nduration precipitation event over the area. 13z NBM is showing \nprobabilities for half an inch or more precipitation accumulation \nat above 80% for most of the area from Friday afternoon through \nSunday morning. Probabilities for an inch or more are above 60% \nfor most of the area. These high probabilities are due to both the\nECMWF and GFS ensembles showing very similar storm tracks and \nmoisture profiles for this weekend. That good agreement is driving\nthe high NBM probabilities, along with the unusually high \natmospheric moisture levels over the region. WPC has a marginal \nrisk for excessive rainfall over the area both Friday and Saturday\nnow. So, plan on a wet weekend, especially Saturday, if you have \noutdoor plans and ave a way to get weather alerts in case flood \nadvisories or warnings become necessary. \n\nGFS ensemble grabs the upper low and quickly shifts it east of \nthe area on Sunday, ending precipitation chances for all but the\nfar eastern zones by early afternoon. ECMWF ensemble is slower\nwith this progression and lingers more widespread precipitation\nchances into Monday morning, before pushing east of the area\nMonday afternoon. NBM combines these and dries out the western\nfoothills Sunday afternoon, but keeps chance or better\nprecipitation chances going over central areas into Sunday night\nwith precipitation chances finally drying up by Monday afternoon\nover Carter/Fallon counties. \n\nThis system continues to look significantly warmer than last\nweekends system. As such, the forecast for snow levels remains at\nthe highest elevations of the Beartooth/Absaroka/Crazy mountains,\nwith forecast accumulations through the weekend staying under 5\ninches. Will continue to monitor the snowfall situation to see if\nimpacts to the Beartooth Highway would necessitate an advisory,\nbut at this time it doesn't appear an advisory will be needed. \nChambers",
